---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WaterIntakeCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/WaterIntakeCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="water-intake-calculator">
  <WaterIntakeCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the water intake calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your body weight. It sets the baseline and matters much more than any rule of thumb.</li>
      <li>Add your age. Kidney concentrating ability declines with age, so the per-kilogram figure tapers.</li>
      <li>Enter about how many minutes a day you exercise or sweat.</li>
      <li>Pick today's climate where you are, not the annual average.</li>
      <li>Read two numbers: total water, and the smaller drinks-only figure that you act on.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Component</th>
            <th scope="col">Rule used</th>
            <th scope="col">Source of the figure</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Baseline, age 18–30</td>
            <td>40 ml per kg</td>
            <td>Standard clinical maintenance-fluid estimate for younger adults</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Baseline, age 31–55</td>
            <td>35 ml per kg</td>
            <td>The most commonly cited adult figure</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Baseline, age 56+</td>
            <td>30 ml per kg</td>
            <td>Reduced renal concentrating capacity and blunted thirst</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Exercise</td>
            <td>+12 ml per minute (≈350 ml per 30 min)</td>
            <td>American College of Sports Medicine guidance on replacing sweat losses</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Climate</td>
            <td>×1.00 temperate up to ×1.25 very hot or humid</td>
            <td>Sweat losses rise steeply with heat, and further when humidity blocks evaporation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pregnancy / breastfeeding</td>
            <td>+300 ml / +700 ml</td>
            <td>EFSA adequate-intake adjustments</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Food contribution</td>
            <td>20% of total</td>
            <td>EFSA and IOM both put food at roughly 20–30% of water intake</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Reference intakes from health authorities</h2>
    <p>
      The two most-cited sets of figures are for total water: drinks plus the water in food.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Authority</th>
            <th scope="col">Adult men</th>
            <th scope="col">Adult women</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>EFSA (European Food Safety Authority)</td>
            <td>2.5 L per day total water</td>
            <td>2.0 L per day total water</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>US National Academies (IOM)</td>
            <td>3.7 L per day total water (about 13 cups from drinks)</td>
            <td>2.7 L per day total water (about 9 cups from drinks)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      They differ because they come from different population survey data. Both are population averages for
      sedentary adults in temperate climates, so an estimate based on body weight, with adjustments, is more
      useful for one person.
    </p>

    <h2>Where "eight glasses a day" came from</h2>
    <p>
      The slogan probably comes from a 1945 US Food and Nutrition Board recommendation of about 2.5 litres a
      day. That recommendation also said "most of this quantity is contained in prepared foods", a sentence
      that was later usually left out. No trial shows that eight glasses is the right target for anyone.
    </p>

    <h2>Does coffee count?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect above about 250–300 mg in people who do not drink it regularly,
      but the water in the drink is much more than the extra urine. Controlled studies that compare coffee with
      equal volumes of water find no meaningful difference in hydration. Tea, milk, juice and soft drinks also
      count.
    </p>
    <p>
      Alcohol is the exception. It suppresses vasopressin, so you lose more fluid than you take in: about 10 ml
      of extra urine per gram of alcohol. Alternate alcoholic drinks with water.
    </p>

    <h2>How to tell whether you are hydrated</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Urine colour: pale straw is the target. Dark yellow or amber means drink more. Colourless all day
        usually means you drink more than you need. B vitamins turn urine bright yellow, which can mislead
        you.
      </li>
      <li>
        Frequency: four to seven trips a day is typical for a well-hydrated adult.
      </li>
      <li>
        Thirst: for healthy adults in normal conditions, drinking when thirsty works. It is less reliable in
        older adults, in extreme heat and during long exercise.
      </li>
      <li>
        Weight around exercise: weigh yourself before and after a long session. Each kilogram lost is about a
        litre of sweat; replace 1.25 to 1.5 litres per kilogram over the next hours.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When plain water is not enough</h2>
    <p>
      Sweat contains sodium: about 0.5 to 1.5 g per litre, and more in people not used to the heat. For efforts
      over about 90 minutes, in heat, or for people who finish sessions with salt on their skin, water without
      sodium dilutes blood sodium and can cause hyponatraemia. Symptoms are headache, nausea, confusion and
      swelling, and in endurance events it is more dangerous than mild dehydration. Use an electrolyte drink,
      or add salt to food after long sessions.
    </p>

    <h2>Habits that work better than a target</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Drink 400–500 ml on waking; you are mildly dehydrated after a night's sleep.</li>
      <li>Keep a filled bottle where you work, where you can see it.</li>
      <li>Attach drinking to existing habits: every meal, every coffee, every time you stand up.</li>
      <li>Drink more early in the day, so you do not catch up at 10 pm and then sleep badly.</li>
      <li>Eat water: cucumber, watermelon, tomatoes, soup and yoghurt are 80 to 95 percent water.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
